# Tool arsenal

Carlos, Nov 18–19 2026: the evaluations exist so that chats and Jarvis know which tools exist and can use all or part of them in daily work. Verdicts are decisions. A status changes only with its ticket (`scripts/ticket.sh`).

## Rules for any outside tool
- Check the tables below first. Use an adopted tool as pinned here. A pilot runs only inside its ticket's scope.
- Provenance first: publisher, license, version. Pin a version or commit. Stars are not evidence: several 2026 repos showed very high stars for their age.
- **Commercial & licensing policy (Nov 19, 2026):**
  - Paid/customer product code uses permissive licenses (`MIT`, `Apache-2.0`, `BSD`, `ISC`, `PDDL`, `CC0`) directly.
  - `CC-BY` / `CC-BY-SA` data (e.g., `co2.js` grid factors, `CERT SG IRM` playbooks) is allowed with required attribution.
  - `MPL-2.0` (`mdn-http-observatory`, `mediabunny`) stays unmodified at file level or is clean-room ported.
  - `AGPL-3.0` and `CC BY-NC` (`Cloudflare Radar API`, `Shodan InternetDB`, `CIRCL vulnerability-lookup`, `listmonk`, `RSSHub`, `postiz-app`) stay strictly external feed reads or separate internal-only tools—never bundled into paid/customer code unless commercially licensed.
- Never install from a community hub (CLI-Anything). Read `setup` or install scripts before running them (REA `setup` writes agent configs).
- Browser-facing scripts/assets under `script-src 'self'` (such as `designs/3d/` and `live.html`) must be self-hosted/vendored, never loaded from external CDNs.
- Any open repo, before a verdict: `node scripts/tool-scout.js repo owner/name` prints its facts (license class, dates, star velocity, owner, README flags, whether it is in this arsenal). It states no verdict; a row changes only with its ticket.
- A new tool gets a ticket. Add its row here when it is adopted or rejected.
- End-user apps (desktop, mobile or personal dashboards, learning material) may be listed for Carlos's personal use at his own responsibility (Nov 19, 2026). They are never embedded in product or customer code.
- Read the LICENSE file, not only GitHub's license badge: the badge shows NOASSERTION for many real MIT and Apache repos (`adobe/trustmark` opens with an Adobe notice and then grants MIT) and shows a license for repos whose datasets carry others (`sapics/ip-location-db`).
- A weekly scout (`scripts/tool-scout.js`, draft PRs named `tool-scout/<date>`, #3857) re-checks every repo here and searches for new ones. Its report is a suggestion: a row is added or changed only with its ticket.
- Tools that make public output follow the rule for that output: `social-clips.mdc` (voice, clips), `scrum-board.mdc` (design assets, images), `media-review.mdc` (media for review).

## Gate for outside skills and MCP servers (#3835, Nov 20 2026)
Before any third-party `.cursor/skills/*`, `SKILL.md` or MCP server is copied in or enabled, run this gate and put the result in the ticket. The first scan is in-repo and dependency-free (`scripts/skill-scan.js`); the two outside scanners are documented steps, not installed or vendored.
1. Provenance: publisher, LICENSE file text, pinned commit. Read every file of the skill, and any `setup` or install script, before running anything.
2. **Required, in-repo:** `node scripts/skill-scan.js <candidate skill dir or mcp.json>` (`npm run skills:scan` with no argument scans our own `.cursor/skills` and every `mcp.json`). It exits 1 and prints `file:line  rule-id` (never the matched text) for: `remote-pipe-shell` (curl or wget into a shell), `encoded-blob` (base64 200+ or hex 128+), `prompt-override`, `hidden-char` (zero-width and bidi), `unknown-host` (not on the `HOSTS` allowlist in the script: github.com, api.github.com, raw.githubusercontent.com, cursor.com, cloudflare.com, developers.cloudflare.com, localhost), `destructive-command`, `curl-post-unknown`. Any finding rejects the skill or server until a human reads it: a real problem is fixed or the skill is dropped; a false positive goes into `ALLOW` in the script with its reason, never a global silence. Adding a host to `HOSTS` needs a reason in the PR. Not wired into CI yet (left in #3835).
3. Deeper scan with `cisco-ai-defense/skill-scanner` (LICENSE reads Apache-2.0, `pyproject.toml` agrees; the GitHub badge shows NOASSERTION; checked at `c558c42`). Run it from a scratch checkout or `uvx`, outside this repo: `skill-scanner scan <skill dir>`. Any high or critical finding (prompt injection, exfiltration, hidden commands) rejects the skill.
4. Overlap check with `NVIDIA/SkillEvaluator` (Apache-2.0, checked at `1dd97c8`), optional, also from a scratch path: skip a skill that duplicates one in `.cursor/skills` or a rule in `.cursor/rules`.
5. MCP servers: list the tools and env vars it asks for, grant no more than the ticket needs, never put a key in the repo, and pin the version.
6. Adopt by adding a row under Adopted with the pin and notice (as `.cursor/skills/THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md` does). Install either scanner into the repo only through its own ticket.

